.TH "CSSMATHMIN.VALUES" "3JS" "August 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathMin.values \- CSSMathMin: values property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]values\f[B]\f[R] read\-only property of the
\f[CR]CSSMathMin\f[R] interface returns a \f[CR]CSSNumericArray\f[R]
containing the \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] objects being compared to find
the minimum.
.SH VALUE
A \f[CR]CSSNumericArray\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathMin\f[R] object and logs its
\f[CR]values\f[R] and length.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] min = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathMin(CSS.px(10), CSS.em(5), CSS.percent(50));

console.log(min.values);
\f[I]// CSSNumericArray {0: CSSUnitValue, 1: CSSUnitValue, 2: CSSUnitValue, length: 3}\f[R]
console.log(min.values.length); \f[I]// 3\f[R]
.EE
.PP
We then iterate over the \f[CR]values\f[R], logging their type, value,
unit, and stringified text.
Each of these matches the \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] objects that were
passed into the constructor (or the operands of the CSS \f[CR]min()\f[R]
function it represents), in the same order.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] value \f[B]of\f[R] min.values) {
  console.log(
    \(ga${value.constructor.name}: ${value.value} ${value.unit} (${value})\(ga,
  );
}

\f[I]// CSSUnitValue: 10 px (10px)\f[R]
\f[I]// CSSUnitValue: 5 em (5em)\f[R]
\f[I]// CSSUnitValue: 50 percent (50%)\f[R]
.EE
